W: 2. Wha's that at the door that is speaking sae canty?
W: 'Tis the voice o' my wee wiﬁe Maggie by name;
W: Come in my dear lassie and sit doon beside me,
W: O rise up, my Johnnie and come awa hame.
W:
W: 3. The bairnies at home, they're a roarin' and greetin',
W: Nae meal in the barrel to fill their wee wame,
W: While ye sit here drinkin' ye leave us lamentin',
W: O rise up, my Johnnie and come awa hame.
W:
W: 4. O Johnnie my dear, when we were first acquainted,
W: Nae ale house nor tavern e'er ran in our minds,
W: But we spent the lang day mang the sweet scented roses,
W: And he'er took thought upon gaun away hame.
W:
W: 5. "O weel do I mind on the time that ye speak o';
W: Those days are awa and will ne'er come again;
W: But as for the present we will try for to mend it;
W: Sae gie's yer hand Maggie and I'll come awa hame."
W:
W: 6. Then Johnnie got up and he banged the door open;
W: Says "Woe to the day to this tavern I came;
W: And fare ye weel whiskey that mak's me aye tipsy;
W: Sae gie's yer hand Maggie and come awa hame."
W:
W: 7. Noo Johnnie gaes out on a fine simmer's evening,
W: Wi' his wife and his bairnies fu' trig and fu' bien,
W: But a short time before in rags they were runnin',
W: While Johnnie sat drinkin' in the ale-hoose at e'en.
%
